--TEST--
Core\Totp is neutral on both qualifier axes: a `secret` goes into `code` and printable text comes out, and a `tainted` code goes into `check` and an ordinary `int` comes back — while the six characters the user typed are still tainted afterwards, because verifying a code is not laundering it — `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key` and `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`, `rule:security/secret-propagation`, `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement across two qualifier axes, asserted by the *declarations* rather
// than by what is printed: neither binding below can be spelled the other way
// round and still compile, so this file compiling at all is the assertion and
// the output is the evidence that the values are the ones being talked about.
// Both rows are `Qual::Neutral` in `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/totp.rs`, and the
// class is unusable without either one — which is exactly why no case had asked
// for the qualifier the class's own registry comments promise.
secret bytes $secret = Core\Random::bytes(20);

// 1. `rule:security/secret-qualifier`'s axis. Showing the six digits to somebody is the whole point of
// the member, and a `secret` value is refused by output, logs and dumps alike —
// so a contagious row here would refuse this declaration and put the member's
// own purpose out of reach. The same shape as `Core\Password::hash`'s answer:
// the second of exactly two operations that take a `secret` and answer
// something that is not one.
string $shown = Core\Totp::code($secret);
echo "a secret goes in and ordinary text comes out: ",
     Core\Str::length($shown) == 6 ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// 2. `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`'s axis, on the member's most common call site: a code arrives out
// of a form, so it is tainted before anything has looked at it. `check`'s
// `$code` is neutral rather than a sink — it accepts what the user typed — and
// the step that comes back was decided by the clock rather than carried by the
// argument, which is what neutral means on a row.
tainted string $typed = $shown;
int $step = Core\Totp::check($typed, $secret) ?? -1;
echo "a tainted code verifies: ", $step > 0 ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// 3. And it is still tainted on the way out, which is `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder` for the
// third roster entry: knowing the secret proves the holder is who they say,
// and says nothing about where the six characters have been. The pattern sink
// refuses the very value `check` just accepted, so laundering it stays a
// separate, written act — as `Core\Jwt::verify`'s claims are, and unlike
// `Core\SignedCookie::open`'s payload, which this program had sealed itself.
string $quoted = Core\Regex::quote($typed);
Core\Regex\Pattern $laundered = Core\Regex::compile($quoted);
echo Core\Regex::matches($shown, $laundered)
     ? "and laundering it is a separate act" : "the pattern lost the code", "\n";

// 4. The answer is an ordinary `int` and not a qualified one, which is the half
// that makes the replay refusal storable at all: a caller writes this number
// beside the account and hands it back, and a value carrying either qualifier
// could not be written to a row or compared against one.
echo Core\Totp::check($typed, $secret, $step) == null
     ? "and the step it answered is a number a caller can keep" : "a replay was accepted", "\n";
--EXPECT--
a secret goes in and ordinary text comes out: yes
a tainted code verifies: yes
and laundering it is a separate act
and the step it answered is a number a caller can keep
